export const fareHarborWinterEditorialOverviews: Record<string, string> = {
  "44195": "Explore the sheltered waters around Marco Island from the small waterfront community of Goodland, a gateway to southwest Florida's intricate coastal islands. This two-hour sightseeing and shelling trip combines time on the water with an introduction to the region's wildlife-rich estuaries and shoreline habitats. Watch the mangrove edges and shallow channels for birds and other coastal animals, remembering that wild creatures appear on their own schedule. Shelling adds another dimension to the outing: the beaches and tidal margins here preserve an ever-changing assortment of shells shaped and sorted by waves and currents. The operator provides shell bags, bottled water, and a cooler with ice for your use. It is an approachable introduction to this remarkable coastline, especially for visitors who want both scenery and a hands-on reason to explore beyond Marco Island's developed beaches.",
  "128312": "Leave the busy waterfront behind and watch southwest Florida change color on a two-hour sunset cruise from Goodland. The route explores the waterways of the Ten Thousand Islands and the protected surroundings of Rookery Bay, where mangrove shorelines shelter an extraordinary variety of coastal life. Keep an eye out for dolphins surfacing near the boat and for wading birds searching the shallows; wildlife sightings depend on the day. Your captain may also pause at a beach where you can look for shells before the evening light settles over the Gulf. This is a particularly rewarding way to understand the region's maze of islands and tidal channels, landscapes that are difficult to appreciate from the road. The boat accommodates groups of up to 17, making the outing well suited to families and friends who want a relaxed coastal experience.",
  "154092": "The barrier islands and tidal beaches near Marco Island are among southwest Florida's most intriguing places to search for shells. This four-hour outing departs from Goodland and gives you time to explore the coastal environment rather than simply glimpse it from shore. Along the way, take in the mangrove-fringed waterways and low islands that define the Ten Thousand Islands region, where shifting tides continually reshape sandbars and beaches. Your focus is shelling, with time to examine the distinctive forms, colors, and textures left by marine mollusks. The operator supplies shell bags, bottled water, and a cooler with ice for guest use. With a maximum group size of 17, the tour offers an accessible way to spend part of your day on the water and discover a side of the Marco Island area that road travelers rarely see.",
  "22901": "See the wetter, greener side of Hawaiʻi Island on a full-day journey from the Kona coast to its dramatic eastern valleys. After crossing Parker Ranch country, stop at Waipiʻo Valley Lookout, where steep volcanic cliffs frame a black-sand shoreline far below. Follow the Hāmākua Coast to ʻAkaka Falls, which drops 442 feet into a rainforest gorge, then explore the lush paths and coastal views of the Hawaiʻi Tropical Bioreserve & Garden at Onomea Bay. The afternoon brings a visit to privately accessible Kulaniapia Falls, where swimming beneath the 120-foot cascade may be possible when water and weather conditions are safe. Near Hilo, see Rainbow Falls and its enormous neighboring banyan tree before returning between Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa on Saddle Road. Expect short walks on occasionally slippery terrain, rich botanical scenery, and an illuminating introduction to the island’s volcanic landscapes and cultural history."
};
